    "durationMs": 347222,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully tested. Summary of what I built:\n\n**Implementation** — Rails 8.0.2 API-only app with `Link` model (unique `code` index, `clicks` counter), `LinksController` (create/show/index) and `RedirectsController` (302 redirect). Codes are 4–20 chars of `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, auto-generated via `SecureRandom.alphanumeric` with collision retry; URLs are validated with `URI` (absolute http/https with host) and returned byte-for-byte unchanged (HTML entity escaping in JSON disabled). Malformed JSON is rescued into a 422, duplicates return 409, unknown codes 404 — all with an `error` field. Clicks increment via an atomic `UPDATE ... clicks = clicks + 1`.\n\n**Notable fix** — the environment's bundler resolved `json 3.1.0`, which removed the `quirks_mode` option Rails 8.0.2's decoder still passes, breaking every JSON request. I pinned `json ~> 2.9.1` in the Gemfile.\n\n**Operations** — `config/database.yml` reads strictly from `DATABASE_URL`; `sh start.sh` removes a stale pid file, runs `rails db:prepare` (create + pending migrations — verified with a temp migration), then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`. README documents startup.\n\n**Verification** — 37/37 assertions pass: 201/409/422/404/302 paths, boundary code lengths, raw `Location` header equal to the stored URL, exactly-one increment per redirect, ascending list order with `total`, and state (data + click counts) identical across a server restart. The DB is left empty and port 8000 is free for the grading run."
  }
